# Speaker Layout Converter

Convert loudspeaker layouts between the file formats used by common spatialization tools.

Each tool stores its speaker configuration in its own format. Speaker Layout Converter reads a
layout from one format and writes it to another, going through a single internal model that keeps
each speaker's name, position, orientation, gain and delay.

## Supported formats

Read and write, in any direction:

| Format | Extension | Token |
|---|---|---|
| SpatGRIS v3 / v4 | `.xml` | `spatgris`, `spatgris_v3`, `spatgris_v4` |
| IEM AllRADecoder | `.json` | `iem` |
| IRCAM Spat / Panoramix | `.rtf` | `spat_ircam` |
| EASE / EASE Focus 3 | `.xld`, `.ease` | `xld`, `ease` |
| 4DSOUND | `.xml` | `4dsound` |
| Flux Spat Revolution | `.ioconfig` | `spat_revolution` |
| CSV (cartesian or spherical) | `.csv` | `csv` |

SpatGRIS export detects dome vs cube layouts and can normalize distances.

## Usage

The same C++ engine ships as a command-line tool, a Qt desktop application and a WebAssembly build.

### CLI (`spatparsecli`)

```
spatparsecli --in-format=<token> --out-format=<token> [--normalize <ratio>] [--center] <input-file> > <output-file>
```

Example:

```
spatparsecli --in-format=ease --out-format=spatgris_v4 room.xld > room_spatgris.xml
```

### GUI

Run the `speaker-layout-converter` application, load a file and pick the output format.

### Web

The WebAssembly build runs the same converter in a browser.

## Build

Requires a C++23 compiler and CMake ≥ 3.25. Qt 6 (Core, Widgets) is needed only for the GUI; the
remaining dependencies are fetched by CMake.

```
cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
cmake --build build
```

## Packaging

Each platform gets the delivery format it expects, built from the same `cmake --install` tree by
one script per platform in `packaging/`. Artifacts are named
`speaker-layout-converter-<version>-<platform>-<arch>.<ext>` and land in `packages/`.

| Platform | Script | Artifact |
|---|---|---|
| Linux | `packaging/build-appimage.sh` | `…-linux-x86_64.AppImage` |
| macOS | `packaging/build-macos-dmg.sh` | `…-macos-arm64.dmg` holding `Speaker Layout Converter.app` |
| Windows | `packaging/build-windows-zip.sh` | `…-windows-x86_64.zip` |

Each artifact carries both the GUI and the CLI. In the AppImage, `spatparsecli` is reached with
`--cli` (or by naming the symlink `spatparsecli`); in the `.app` it is
`Contents/MacOS/spatparsecli`; in the zip it sits next to the GUI executable.

All three scripts refuse to package a build whose binaries resolve libraries outside the system
prefix, so a tree that would only run on the machine that built it fails the build instead of
shipping.

### macOS signing

`build-macos-dmg.sh` signs when `MAC_CODESIGN_IDENTITY` is set and notarizes when
`NOTARY_PROFILE` is also set, following the same invocation as score's
`ci/osx.package.deploy.sh`: every Mach-O in the bundle is signed inside out with
`--options=runtime --timestamp`, then the bundle itself with
`packaging/entitlements.plist`, then the `.dmg`. Without those variables it produces an
unsigned artifact and says so rather than looking like it signed one.

## Status

Early stage, single maintainer. No versioned release yet — builds are published as CI artifacts.
A LICENSE file is being added (see #4).
